.TH "GIT-CL" "1" "2026-08-16" "pylitfun" "Git-Verbs Manual"
.SH NAME
git-cl \- remove untracked files from the working tree
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git cl
.RB [ \-n ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B git cl
deletes every file and directory that
.B git status \-\-ignored
would list: every file and directory not under version control, including ones listed only by
.BR .gitignore .
It runs
.B git clean \-dffxq
to do this.
.PP
Because deleting is hard to reverse,
.B git cl
prints the command it is about to run, then blocks reading Stdin until you press Control-D to confirm (or Control-C to cancel), before it deletes anything.
.SH OPTIONS
.PP
\-n
.RS 4
Dry run. Runs
.B git clean \-dffxn
right away, with no confirmation prompt, since a dry run deletes nothing.
.PP
Recognized only when
.B \-n
is the one and only argument. Any other argument list falls back to the confirmed form above, forwarding those arguments to
.BR "git clean \-dffxq" .
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
Exits with the status of the
.B git clean
it ran.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
git cl
.RS 4
Confirm, then delete every untracked file and directory.
.RE
.PP
git cl \-n
.RS 4
Show what would be deleted, without deleting anything and without asking to confirm.
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git-clean (1),
.BR git-status (1)
